<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Dobunni</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 43 CE (approx.) · capital Bagendon; later Corinium Dobunnorum (Cirencester)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Cotswold kingdom whose coins name a string of rulers, and whose Roman town at Cirencester became the second largest in Britain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dobunni held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. By the 1st century BCE they were minting coins, and a large oppidum developed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagendon">Bagendon</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a>, with Roman imports reaching it before the conquest.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins carry the names of rulers such as Corio, Bodvoc, Anted and Eisu, sometimes thought to mark a divided kingdom with northern and southern halves. The economy rested on sheep and cereal farming in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a> and on the salt springs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/droitwich-spa">Droitwich</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> records that part of the Bodunni, probably the Dobunni, had been subject to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 43 part of the Dobunni surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-plautius">Aulus Plautius</a>, and the region came under Roman control without major recorded conflict. A fort was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a>, and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinium-dobunnorum">Corinium Dobunnorum</a> grew into the civitas capital and second largest town of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dobunni.jpg" alt="Dobunni" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Jpb1301 de... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Dobunni.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 43 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bagendon; later Corinium Dobunnorum (Cirencester)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Corio, Bodvoc, Eisu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Boduni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-4&amp;focus=dobunni" aria-label="Dobunni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4624 367 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dobunni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4944 623L5304 942M4944 623L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4944 623h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4944 623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-4&amp;focus=dobunni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
